# The British Conquest and the Revolt of 1857

> A chartered trading company became the subcontinent's ruler in stages —
> factories, Carnatic proxies, Plassey's conspiracy, Buxar's legitimacy, and
> the tools of Subsidiary Alliance and Lapse. In 1857 the sepoys rose; the
> revolt failed, but it ended Company rule and remade the Raj.

## 1. The Company Arrives (1600-1690)

Queen Elizabeth I chartered the **East India Company on 31 December 1600**.
Embassies to Jahangir's court (Hawkins 1608, Sir Thomas Roe 1615) won a
farman, and the **Surat factory opened in 1613**.

| Settlement | Year | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Surat | 1613 | Jahangir's farman after Roe's embassy |
| Masulipatnam | 1611 | First eastern factory |
| Madras | 1639 | Francis Day builds Fort St. George |
| Bombay | 1661 | Dowry of Catherine of Braganza; leased to the Company 1668 |
| Calcutta | 1690 | Job Charnock founds it; Fort William follows |

## 2. Lessons of the Carnatic

The three **Carnatic wars (1746-63)** taught the English that Indian
succession disputes could be won by chequebook and discipline: Dupleix's
French proxies fell to Clive's defence of Arcot, and **Wandiwash (1760)**
left Britain's company the only European power that mattered — while
Indian rulers learned too late that the Europeans were now players, not
pedlars.

## 3. Bengal: Plassey, Buxar, and the Diwani

- **Plassey, 23 June 1757:** after Siraj-ud-Daulah seized Calcutta, Robert
  **Clive** won a battle decided before it began — the Nawab's general
  **Mir Jafar** had been bought, with the Jagat Seth bankers brokering.
  Bengal's treasury now bankrolled the Company.
- **Buxar, 22 October 1764:** Hector Munro defeated the "triple alliance"
  of **Mir Qasim**, Awadh's **Shuja-ud-Daula**, and Emperor **Shah Alam
  II** — a victory over sovereigns, not a merchant skirmish.
- **Treaty of Allahabad (1765):** the Emperor granted the Company the
  **Diwani** — revenue rights of Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa. The "dual
  government" (Company revenue, Nawab's administration) ran until Warren
  Hastings ended it in 1772.

[definition]
The **Diwani of 1765** made the Company the revenue farmer of eastern
India — the legal cover for conquest. For the next century, Indian land
revenue, not English capital, paid for Indian expansion.
[/definition]

## 4. Wars of Conquest: Mysore, Marathas, Sikhs

| Series | Wars | End |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Anglo-Mysore | 4 (1767-99) | Tipu Sultan dies at Seringapatam, 4 May 1799; his war rockets later shaped British designs |
| Anglo-Maratha | 3 (1775-1818) | Peshwaship abolished; Baji Rao II pensioned at Bithur, 1818 |
| Anglo-Sikh | 2 (1845-49) | Punjab annexed by Dalhousie, 29 March 1849; Kohinoor goes to Queen Victoria |

**Haider Ali** and his son **Tipu Sultan** — the "Tiger of Mysore" —
modernised army and rocket corps and allied with the French; the **Treaty
of Seringapatam (1792)** halved Mysore after Cornwallis's campaign.
**Ranjit Singh's** Sikh kingdom (Lahore, 1799-1839) fell within a decade of
his death.

## 5. The Governor-Generals and Their Tools

| Governor-General | Years | Signature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Warren Hastings | 1774-85 | Regulating Act 1773's first appointee; ends dual government |
| Cornwallis | 1786-93 | Permanent Settlement of Bengal, 1793 |
| Wellesley | 1798-1805 | Subsidiary Alliances; Fort William College |
| William Bentinck | 1828-35 | Sati abolished 1829; English education 1835 |
| Dalhousie | 1848-56 | Railways and telegraph; Doctrine of Lapse |

- **Subsidiary Alliance (Wellesley, 1798):** a ruler accepted a British
  resident and paid for Company troops "stationed" for protection, ending
  his independent diplomacy — Hyderabad signed first (1798), then Mysore
  and Awadh (1801).
- **Doctrine of Lapse (Dalhousie):** adopted heirs could not inherit a
  dependent state — **Satara (1848), Jaitpur and Sambalpur (1849), Jhansi
  (1853), Nagpur (1854)** were annexed. In 1856 **Awadh itself** fell on a
  charge of misgovernment.

## 6. Why the Revolt of 1857 Happened

| Category | Grievance |
| --- | --- |
| Political | Annexations; Nana Saheb's pension and Jhansi's adoption denied; Bahadur Shah's successors told to quit the Red Fort |
| Economic | Heavy land revenue, ruin of artisans, resumption of tax-free lands |
| Socio-religious | Fear of conversion; 1850 act letting converts keep inheritance taxed as aggression; taxes on temple and mosque lands |
| Military | Sepoys outnumbered Europeans 6 to 1; pay and promotion resentment; General Service Enlistment Act 1856 (overseas service) |
| Immediate | Enfield rifle's cartridges greased, it was rumoured, with cow and pig fat, to be bitten before loading |

On **29 March 1857** at Barrackpore, **Mangal Pandey** attacked his
officers and was hanged; on **10 May** the Meerut garrison mutinied and
rode through the night to Delhi, where the aged **Bahadur Shah Zafar** was
proclaimed emperor.

## 7. The Course of the Revolt

@diagram tree id="revolt-centres" layout="tree" aria="Main centres of the 1857 revolt and their leaders"
  Revolt of 1857 -> "Delhi: Bahadur Shah Zafar, Bakht Khan"
  Revolt of 1857 -> "Kanpur: Nana Saheb, Tantia Tope"
  Revolt of 1857 -> "Lucknow: Begum Hazrat Mahal"
  Revolt of 1857 -> "Jhansi: Rani Lakshmibai, Tantia Tope"
  Revolt of 1857 -> "Bihar: Kunwar Singh"
@/diagram

| Centre | Leader(s) | Fate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Delhi | Bahadur Shah Zafar, Bakht Khan | Recaptured 20 Sept 1857; princes shot; emperor exiled |
| Kanpur | Nana Saheb, Tantia Tope | Nana escaped to Nepal; Tantia executed 1859 |
| Lucknow | Begum Hazrat Mahal for Birjis Qadir | Residency siege; city retaken March 1858 |
| Jhansi | Rani Lakshmibai | Died in battle, June 1858 — "the best and bravest" (Hugh Rose) |
| Jagdishpur | Kunwar Singh, aged 80 | Fought on until his death in 1858 |

## 8. Why It Failed and What Changed

The revolt stayed north-central: the Punjab's Sikhs, Nepal's Gurkhas, most
Maratha houses, Hyderabad, and the educated urban classes held aloof —
and the telegraph, the Enfield, and naval reinforcement decided the
sieve-work of the cities.

- **Government of India Act 1858:** the Crown took over; a Secretary of
  State in London with a 15-member council; the Governor-General became
  **Viceroy** (Canning the first).
- **Queen's Proclamation, 1 November 1858** (read at Allahabad): the
  "Magna Carta" of the Indian people — no more annexations, religious
  non-interference, equal protection.
- **Army rebuilt:** artillery reserved to Europeans, mixed recruitment,
  and the "martial races" of the Punjab preferred.
- The **Mughal title ended**: Bahadur Shah, tried in the Red Fort's Diwan-i
  Khas, died in Rangoon in 1862.

[summary]
1857 was a sepoy mutiny that became a popular rising — but not yet a
national one: no common command, no modern arms, and a divided country
gave the Company's last campaign its victory. The Company died with its
conquest: the Raj ruled in the Queen's name for the next ninety years.
[/summary]

## Key Takeaways

- The Company moved from Surat's factory (1613) to the Diwani of 1765 by
  naval war, money, and conspiracy — Plassey bought, Buxar fought.
- Four Mysore wars, three Maratha wars, and two Sikh wars (ending 1849)
  completed conquest, greased by Subsidiary Alliances and the Doctrine of
  Lapse.
- 1857 combined political, economic, religious, and military grievances,
  ignited by the greased Enfield cartridge.
- Its failure brought Crown rule, a new army, the Queen's Proclamation —
  and the political quiet before the national movement.
